'''testamental''' (tes-tạ-men′tạl), ''a.'' [< LL. ''testamentalis'', of or pertaining to a will, < L. ''testamentum'', a will: see ''testament.''] Relating to or of the nature of a testament or will; testamentary.

{{smaller block|The ''testamental'' cup I take,<br>And thus remember thee.}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Montgomery'', According to thy gracious word.}}}}

'''testamentarily''' (tes-tạ-men′tạ-ri-li), ''adv.'' By testament or will.

{{smaller block|The children . . . were turned out ''testamentarily.''
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Cripps the Carrier, i.}}}}

'''testamentary''' (tes-tạ-men′tạ-ri), ''a.'' [= F. ''testamentaire'' = Sp. Pg. It. ''testamentario'', < L. ''testamentarius'', of or belonging to a will, < ''testamentum'', a will: see ''testament.''] '''1.''' Relating or pertaining to a will or wills; also, relating to administration of the estates of deceased persons.

{{smaller block|He is in the mater as souverain juge and ordinarie principalle under the Pope in a cause ''testamentarie'', and also by cause the wille of my said Lord is aproved in his court before his predecessour. {{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 373.}}}}

{{smaller block|This spiritual jurisdiction of ''testamentary'' causes is a peculiar constitution of this island; for in almost all other (even in popish) countries all matters ''testamentary'' are under the jurisdiction of the civil magistrate.
{{right|''Blackstone'', Com., III. vii.}}}}

'''2.''' Given or bequeathed by will.

{{smaller block|How many ''testamentary'' charities have been defeated by the negligence or fraud of executors! {{right|''Bp. Atterbury.''}}}}

'''3.''' Set forth or contained in a will.

{{smaller block|To see whether the portrait of their ancestor still keeps its place upon the wall, in compliance with his ''testamentary'' directions. {{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, xviii.}}}}

